1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:00,625
Part three.

2
00:00:01,038 --> 00:00:02,275
Adoption, or pressure?

3
00:00:02,712 --> 00:00:05,575
Bartlett lays out the strongest case against Zitron.

4
00:00:05,988 --> 00:00:09,000
ChatGPT hit a hundred million users in about

5
00:00:09,012 --> 00:00:09,825
sixty days.

6
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:11,538
TikTok took nine months.

7
00:00:11,950 --> 00:00:13,875
Instagram, two and a half years.

8
00:00:14,288 --> 00:00:15,112
In his own company,

9
00:00:15,475 --> 00:00:18,725
he says, nearly everyone uses AI every day.

10
00:00:19,138 --> 00:00:21,675
Zitron's reply is one of his sharpest lines.

11
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:24,900
He calls it the largest non consensual push

12
00:00:24,912 --> 00:00:26,288
of technology in history.

13
00:00:26,712 --> 00:00:27,638
Open Google Docs,

14
00:00:27,862 --> 00:00:28,912
and Gemini is there.

15
00:00:29,325 --> 00:00:31,388
Open Word, and Copilot is waiting.

16
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,075
Even shopping comes with an AI opinion about

17
00:00:34,088 --> 00:00:34,750
your socks.

18
00:00:35,162 --> 00:00:36,137
And for three years,

19
00:00:36,338 --> 00:00:36,875
he says,

20
00:00:37,238 --> 00:00:39,150
the media has told everyone that if they

21
00:00:39,162 --> 00:00:39,888
don't use it,

22
00:00:40,188 --> 00:00:41,100
they'll fall behind.

23
00:00:41,512 --> 00:00:43,700
What about all the AI content flooding the

24
00:00:43,712 --> 00:00:44,238
internet?

25
00:00:44,650 --> 00:00:46,462
Zitron says we already had slop.

26
00:00:46,875 --> 00:00:47,788
Search engine slop.

27
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:49,688
AI just gave us a slop machine.

28
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,575
Then they argue about mistakes.

29
00:00:52,038 --> 00:00:55,300
Bartlett cites a leaderboard showing hallucination rates on

30
00:00:55,312 --> 00:00:58,162
simple summaries falling from about twenty two percent

31
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:00,550
to under one percent in four years.

32
00:01:00,962 --> 00:01:02,738
Zitron counters with his own test:

33
00:01:03,038 --> 00:01:05,538
an AI tool once told him Microsoft stock

34
00:01:05,688 --> 00:01:08,050
had hit five hundred and seventy five dollars.

35
00:01:08,462 --> 00:01:09,150
It never had.

36
00:01:09,575 --> 00:01:10,688
Harmless in a newsletter,

37
00:01:11,038 --> 00:01:11,575
he says.

38
00:01:11,988 --> 00:01:14,475
Dangerous in a medical transcript or a hedge

39
00:01:14,488 --> 00:01:15,112
fund model.

40
00:01:15,525 --> 00:01:17,075
And his test for real breakthroughs?

41
00:01:17,488 --> 00:01:18,038
The iPhone.

42
00:01:18,462 --> 00:01:19,150
When it launched,

43
00:01:19,538 --> 00:01:21,562
he says, you didn't need to explain it.

44
00:01:21,975 --> 00:01:23,062
You handed it to someone,

45
00:01:23,350 --> 00:01:24,300
and they got it instantly.

46
00:01:24,725 --> 00:01:25,300
With AI,

47
00:01:25,662 --> 00:01:28,588
he argues, people still need a complicated setup

48
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:30,688
of prompts and harnesses to make it work.

49
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:33,962
Bartlett's counterpoint is that we should compare AI

50
00:01:33,975 --> 00:01:35,125
to a human intern,

51
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:36,388
not to perfection.

52
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:37,612
Zitron's answer:

53
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:39,188
a good intern learns,

54
00:01:39,375 --> 00:01:41,062
gets context, and grows.

55
00:01:41,475 --> 00:01:42,038
A chatbot,

56
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:44,262
he says, just reads its files.

57
00:01:44,688 --> 00:01:45,250
To settle it,

58
00:01:45,538 --> 00:01:47,325
Bartlett brings out a deck of cards.
